pancake is one library you can hold three ways. It is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/)
package you import directly, a plain C ABI you can call from any FFI host, and four
language bindings
that wrap that C ABI in a native idiom. Same core underneath, and the surface you pick
never changes the bytes a session puts on the wire, so you choose whichever matches the
language you are writing in.

[One library, held three ways. Zig imports the core directly; C and FFI hosts call the C ABI; the four bindings wrap that same C ABI. One core underneath all of them, so the surface never changes what a session puts on the wire.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-surfaces.svg'))

| Surface | You write | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Zig package]($link.page('start')) | Zig | you are in Zig, or want the full engine and its types |
| C ABI | C, or any FFI host | you are embedding in C, or wrapping from a runtime with no binding yet |
| [Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) | Rust, TypeScript, Python, Racket | you are in one of those four languages |

## The Zig package

`zig fetch --save git+https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake`, import `pancake`,
and you have the application surface, the owning `Connection` and its small verb
vocabulary, plus everything beneath it, down to the raw engine. The
[Start building]($link.page('start')) page walks a first round-trip line by line,
and the [examples](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/README.md)
read top-down from that surface to the engine, one `step` at a time. The exact
declarations you import, with their ownership and lifetime notes, are the generated
[Zig API reference]($link.page('reference/api')).

## The C ABI

The same code compiles to a set of composable archives, linked à la carte, each in
static (`.a`) and shared (`.so`) form: `libpancake_ocapn` carries the sans-io peer
and the shared vocabulary in `pancake_ocapn.h`, and each transport is its own archive
and header (`libpancake_net_transport_tcp` with `pancake_net_transport_tcp.h`,
`libpancake_net_transport_inproc` with `pancake_net_transport_inproc.h`). The surface is
deliberately boring: opaque handles, fixed-width return codes, `ptr + len` views,
callbacks, and explicit release functions. That is what lets a consumer wrap it well
without knowing any Zig. Each header is generated from the same Zig declarations the
archive compiles, so it can never silently disagree with the binary, and the optional
iroh transport adds `pancake_net_transport_iroh.h` as a companion (it includes `pancake_ocapn.h` and
adds only the `pancake_iroh_*` family; a consumer links it alongside the core archives).
[Consuming pancake from C](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md)
walks the build, the artifacts, the ownership rules, and a first complete program.

## The bindings

A binding already did the C wrapping and presents an idiomatic API. See
[Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) for the shared shape, per-language tours, and how
each one dogfoods the archives on every build.

## One library, one build, one engine

There is no separate "C build" or "Zig build" of the protocol. The bindings call
the same C ABI that wraps the same Zig core, so the surface never changes behavior or
the bytes on the wire: a Zig peer, a C consumer, and a peer written against any binding
hold the same OCapN conversation, byte for byte. What a session does *negotiate*, its
codec and CapTP version, is settled per session against whoever answers, not by which
surface you built on, so one instance can run many sessions speaking different codecs at
once; the [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) page walks that handshake. The surfaces
also come from one build: every artifact above is produced by `zig build`, and the
single command `zig build check` proves the whole tree at once: the gate, examples,
dogfoods, and interop. The full step catalog lives in
[the build catalog](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/README.md#build).

The one place the surfaces diverge is encryption: iroh rides a separate optional
artifact, which the [Transports]($link.page('transports')) page covers.
